WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.224
Failure mode and effects analysis, often
called F M E A,

2
00:00:04.374 --> 00:00:06.081
can organize a discussion.

3
00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:10.049
This course uses a fictional three-level
scale for severity,

4
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:12.567
occurrence and difficulty of detection.

5
00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:17.389
These numbers are ordinal teaching
categories, not measured probabilities.

6
00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:19.971
They are not official regulatory limits.

7
00:00:20.121 --> 00:00:24.644
The interactive exercise makes the
arithmetic visible while keeping the

8
00:00:24.794 --> 00:00:29.423
rationale and unresolved high-severity
concerns beside the result.

9
00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:34.589
In this exercise, higher severity means
more serious potential harm.

10
00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:39.091
Higher occurrence means the failure is
more plausible given the available

11
00:00:39.241 --> 00:00:40.323
evidence.

12
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:44.932
Higher detection score means it is harder
to discover the failure before

13
00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:46.234
its consequence.

14
00:00:46.384 --> 00:00:51.056
Real projects need operational definitions
tailored to the scenario.

15
00:00:51.206 --> 00:00:54.299
A team should agree those definitions
before scoring.

16
00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:58.865
The labels lower and higher alone are not
sufficient for an approved site

17
00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:00.096
assessment.

18
00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:04.281
Multiply three by one by two and the
result is six.

19
00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:08.420
Multiply one by three by two and the
result is also six.

20
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.005
Yet the first scenario has a higher
severity category.

21
00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:16.294
The chart shows equal total scores with
different component patterns.

22
00:01:16.444 --> 00:01:21.137
Equal products do not establish equivalent
risk or acceptable controls.

23
00:01:21.287 --> 00:01:26.087
Review the scenario, severity, uncertainty
and control options rather than

24
00:01:26.237 --> 00:01:27.880
letting one number decide.

25
00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.318
Suppose an independent alarm is proposed.

26
00:01:31.468 --> 00:01:35.926
Do not lower the detection score merely
because the alarm appears on a

27
00:01:36.076 --> 00:01:37.156
drawing.

28
00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:40.165
Determine whether it detects the relevant
failure,

29
00:01:40.315 --> 00:01:44.240
whether someone will respond and whether
that response is timely.

30
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.761
Verify the control and reassess residual
risk.

31
00:01:47.911 --> 00:01:52.284
The activity asks you to compare two
scoring rationales and explain which

32
00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.120
assumptions need evidence.

33
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.918
No score in this lesson automatically
authorizes release.
